      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:13 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Based upon years of experience, study, hearings, and overwhelming persuasive evidence, Congress concluded that the prompt removal of aliens convicted of committing serious felonies was essential to the Nation's ability to control its borders."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "an amicus has raised a jurisdictional question"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the test that the Court has consistently applied in this area is there -- is there a rational basis, is the congressional objective rationally likely to advance a legitimate governmental purpose."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But, General Olson, those statistics go to the likelihood of entry of the order of deportation, not of the likelihood of flight which this statute is directed at."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But why can't you deal with that problem with a standard that's tough, that's different from having the hearing? After all, we give bail pending appeal to criminals who have been convicted. We give bail to alien terrorists who are about to be deported. Why couldn't you have a tough standard but, nonetheless -- like bail pending appeal, but, nonetheless, give the bail hearing to the person who's willing to come in and he'd have to show, you know, he's not going to run away, he's not a danger, and he has a good issue on the merits?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: And they can get a hearing on whether they are lawful resident aliens."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. RABINOVITZ: The point is that this statute requires the Government to detain individuals like our client who are lawful permanent residents not because their detention is necessary to protect the public from danger of flight risk, but merely because they were convicted in the past for one of a broad range of crimes that the Government believes may render them deportable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice Kennedy's question about have they been -- is this just that they're suspected of committing crimes or have they been convicted of crimes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Citizens and Immigrants for Equal Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0002-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I refer you to the -- the amicus brief for -- by Citizens and -- and Immigrants for Equal Justice. It's one of these green briefs. And it's on page 12 of their brief."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: You mean the first degree burglary conviction --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Seventh Circuit",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0002-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Seventh Circuit disagreed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Rabinovitz",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0002-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. RABINOVITZ: -- the courts have held that even misdemeanors can be aggravated felonies."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: General Olson, I don't want to intrude upon your rebuttal time, but I have one question that's very important for me and you can answer it yes or no. Assuming I disagree with you as to the reading of the statute as to whether there is jurisdiction in this case, if there is no jurisdiction, is that provision of the statute in the view of the Government unconstitutional?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: The case is submitted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source",
      "case_id": "2002-01-1491",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1af832f80e08b1353f2f3283b8b038009c165c1ffa36ab0520c637ed7c4c6607",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1af832f80e08-0003-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "85 percent of the aliens that are brought into these procedures don't even challenge the immigration decision -- immigration judge decision, and more than half of those cases are resolved within 30 days. The statistics are in the brief."
    }
